</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>Results on instruction-tuning tasks with LLaMA-3.2-3B with different multimodal teacher models. We also highlight the RougeL score when distilled from language-only LLaMA-8B model with SeqKD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim and Rush, 2016</a>)</cite>. A p-value of <math id="S5.T12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.005</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.005</annotation></semantics></math> with one-sided t-test suggests that <span id="S5.T12.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARMADA</span> provides better post-distillation performance than SeqKD. Interestingly, the teacher models used with <span id="S5.T12.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARMADA</span> are significantly smaller (&lt;2B) than the language-only teacher model (8B) used in SeqKD. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Table </span><a href="#S5.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ 5.2 Results on Instruction-tuning Tasks ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ From Images to Words: Efficient Cross-Modal Knowledge Distillation to Language Models from Black-box Teachers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> highlights the performance of LLaMA-3.2-3B-Instruct distilled from different multimodal teacher models with </span><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ARMADA</span><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Across all five tasks, </span><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ARMADA</span><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> demonstrates consistent performance improvement over the undistilled LLaMA-3B, with maximum gains of </span><math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.8</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.8\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.6</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.6\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and </span><math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.5</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> on Dolly, UNI, and SNI tasks, respectively. Surprisingly, </span><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ARMADA</span><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> also outperforms SeqKD (unimodal distillation from LLaMA-8B teacher) by over </span><math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> margin in several tasks like Dolly, SelfInst, and UNI, albeit using significantly smaller teacher models. Among the different modalities, we observe the highest average improvement of </span><math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.4</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.4\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with the text-to-audio teacher model, followed by </span><math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.3</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.3\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> improvement by the text-to-image teacher model. However, ANOVA tests suggest that there might not be any apparent difference between the different teacher modalities in terms of the effectiveness of the LLaMA-3B student model.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Figure </span><a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 Results on Instruction-tuning Tasks ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ From Images to Words: Efficient Cross-Modal Knowledge Distillation to Language Models from Black-box Teachers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> highlights the importance of the manifold alignment weight </span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and auxiliary output weight </span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Except for Dolly evaluation tasks, we observe </span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.5</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> additional benefit in other instruction-tuning tasks due to both hyperparameters. Moreover, ANOVA tests suggest that both the hyperparameters are statistically important (p-value &lt; 0.01) for the higher performance of </span><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ARMADA</span><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Figure </span><a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.2 Results on Instruction-tuning Tasks ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ From Images to Words: Efficient Cross-Modal Knowledge Distillation to Language Models from Black-box Teachers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> illustrates the impact of different manifold alignment loss objectives on the student model. On instruction-tuning tasks, under </span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cosine}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">cosine</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cosine}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ARMADA</span><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> exhibits the highest performance (average improvement of </span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.9</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">), followed by both </span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{elementwise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">elementwise</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{elementwise}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{euclid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">euclid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{euclid}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (</span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.5</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). However, ANOVA test results suggest that all the loss objectives are equally effective (as we fail to reject the null hypothesis stating that the loss functions are all equally performant under a significance level of </span><math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\%</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10877v1/all_ablation_llama3.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:469/253;" width="469" height="253"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Distribution of improvement between the undistilled and <span id="S5.F5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARMADA</span>-distilled LLaMA-3B, for different values of <math id="S5.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. A two-way ANOVA using ordinary least square (OLS) regression model confirms the statistical significance (p-value <math id="S5.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7e-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7e-5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5e-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5e-3</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.F5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively) of both the hyperparameters.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10877v1/all_ablation_llama3_loss.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:272/237;" width="272" height="237"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Distribution of improvement between the undistilled and <span id="S5.F6.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARMADA</span>-distilled LLaMA-3B, for different manifold alignment loss functions. A p-value of <math id="S5.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.52</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.52</annotation></semantics></math> with the ANOVA test suggests that we may not reject the null hypothesis of dissimilarity between the different loss functions. Therefore, the empirical evidence suggests that all the loss functions could be equally effective in the <span id="S5.F6.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARMADA</span> framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
